 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "tristan_island",
   "title": "Tristan da Cunha",
   "text": "The most remote inhabited island in the world: about 2,400 kilometres from St Helena and 2,800 from Cape Town, the nearest city. A Portuguese sailor, Tristao da Cunha, saw it in 1506. The island is the tip of a volcano rising from the floor of the Atlantic, and the settlement is on its only flat land."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "tristan_edinburgh",
   "title": "Edinburgh of the Seven Seas",
   "text": "The only settlement, named after Prince Alfred, Duke of Edinburgh, Queen Victoria's second son, who visited in 1867, the first royal to do so. The islanders call it, simply, the Settlement. It has about 250 people, a hospital, a school, a church, a pub and one shop."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "tristan_glass",
   "title": "William Glass",
   "text": "In 1816 Britain garrisoned Tristan to stop anyone using it to rescue Napoleon from St Helena. When the garrison left in 1817, a Scottish corporal, William Glass of Kelso, stayed on with his wife and children. He founded the community, and Glass is still one of the island's seven surnames."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "tristan_surnames",
   "title": "Seven Surnames",
   "text": "Everyone on Tristan carries one of seven surnames: Glass, Green, Hagan, Lavarello, Repetto, Rogers and Swain. They come from a Scot, a Dutchman, two Italian sailors shipwrecked in 1892, an American whaler and others. The islanders have a shared sense of humour about it."
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "tristan_eruption",
   "title": "The Eruption of 1961",
   "text": "In October 1961 earthquakes shook the settlement, and a new volcano opened right next to it. Every islander was evacuated, by boat to Nightingale Island, then to Cape Town and on to England, where they lived for two years near Southampton. In 1963 most chose to return, and found their houses still standing."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "tristan_potatoes",
   "title": "The Potato Patches",
   "text": "The islanders' traditional farm is the Patches, a set of walled fields west of the Settlement where the potatoes grow. Tristan potatoes are eaten at nearly every meal. The soil is rich and volcanic, and the wind is strong enough to take your cap and carry it to St Helena."
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "tristan_crawfish",
   "title": "The Crawfish",
   "text": "Tristan rock lobster, called crawfish by the islanders, is the island's main source of income. The fishery has been carefully managed, with a quota and a season, for decades. The catch is processed at the factory here and shipped out to markets abroad, in the holds of a ship that visits only a few times a year."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "tristan_rockhoppers",
   "title": "Rockhopper Penguins",
   "text": "Northern rockhopper penguins nest on Tristan and its neighbours, Nightingale and Gough. They hop, rather than waddle, from rock to rock, with both feet together. Their numbers have crashed over the last century, and they are now an endangered species, so the islanders protect their colonies."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 41,
   "plaque": "tristan_peak",
   "title": "Queen Mary's Peak",
   "text": "The island's summit, 2,062 metres, rises almost straight out of the sea, and in winter is white with snow. It is the volcano itself, with a crater lake near the top. There are paths to the summit, but the islanders say you should never go without telling someone and leaving before the cloud comes in."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "tristan_gough",
   "title": "Gough and Inaccessible",
   "text": "Gough Island and Inaccessible Island, in the Tristan da Cunha group, are a World Heritage Site for their seabirds and endemic species. Inaccessible has the world's smallest flightless bird, the Inaccessible Island rail, and Gough has one of the great albatross colonies. Both are protected, and visitors need a permit."
  }
 ],
